(a) The procedures provided for in this section for the owners of dangerous dogs must be carried out as a necessary condition for the enforcement of the provisions of this division against such dangerous dog owners. The procedures provided for in this section for the owners of vicious dogs shall not be an essential element of any crime provided for in this article.
(b) When a vicious or dangerous dog is classified as such, the dog control officer shall notify the dog's owner of such classification.
(c) The classification notice to the owner of such vicious or dangerous dog shall:
(1) Be in writing and mailed by certified mail to the dog owner's last known address;
(2) Include a summary of the dog control officer's findings that formed the basis for the dog's classification as a vicious or dangerous dog;
(3) Be dated and shall state that the dog's owner, within ten days after the date shown on the notice, has a right to request a hearing on the dog control officer's determination that such dog is a vicious or dangerous dog;
(4) State that the hearing, if requested, shall be before the county magistrate court and shall specify such court as the applicable agency which will conduct the hearing;
(5) State that if a hearing is not requested, the dog control officer's determination that such dog is a vicious or dangerous dog will become effective for all purposes under this article on a date specified in the notice, which shall be after the last day on which the owner has a right to request a hearing;
(6) Include a form to request a hearing before the court and shall provide specific instructions on mailing or delivering such request to the court;
(7) Provide that the dog's owner shall submit with the written notice of appeal a check or money order in the amount of forty-nine dollars and fifty cents ($49.50) for the court costs of such appeal and a warning that the dog's owner will be liable for all costs and expenses if the appeal is denied.
(d) When the court receives a request for a hearing as provided in subsection (c) of this section, it shall schedule such hearing within 30 days after receiving the request. The court shall notify the dog's owner, in writing, by certified mail, of the date, time and place of the hearing, and such notice shall be mailed to the dog's owner at least ten days prior to the date of the hearing. At the hearing, the owner of the dog shall be given the opportunity to testify and present evidence, and the court shall receive such other evidence and hear such other testimony as may be reasonably necessary to make a determination either to sustain, modify or overrule the dog control officer's classification of such dog.
(e) Within ten days after the date of the hearing, the court shall notify the dog's owner, in writing, by certified mail, of its determination on the matter. If such determination is that the dog is a vicious or dangerous dog, the notice shall specify the date upon which that determination is effective.
(f) If the determination by the court is that such dog is a vicious or dangerous dog, the owner of such dog shall pay to the general fund of the county the additional sum of seventy-five dollars ($75.00) within 30 days of such determination to defray the costs to the county.
(Ord. No. O-1-95, § 8.00, 7-6-1995)

